92186045417 true] [17592186121032 1064 "The **real coordinate space** (of dimension $n$) is the set of all possible column vectors of height $n,$ whose components are real numbers. Symbolically, we represent the real coordinate space of dimension $n$ as $\\mathbb{R}^n.$\n\nFor example, the following vectors are in $\\mathbb{R}, \\mathbb{R}^2,$ $\\mathbb{R}^3,$ and $\\mathbb{R}^5,$ respectively: \n\n![](images/20/20e0adb04d7a8711cad4a9ee7ad26755d52176fc9549d7809fe10665a8118945.svg)\n\nWe've already encountered several operations on vectors in $\\mathbb{R}^2$ or $\\mathbb{R}^3,$ namely, vector addition and subtraction, scalar multiplication, and the dot product. All of these operations work the same way in $\\mathbb{R}^n.$" 13194139534129 17592186045417 true] [17592186121032 1064 "The **real coordinate space** (of dimension $n$) is the set of all possible column vectors of height $n,$ whose components are real numbers. Symbolically, we represent the real coordinate space of dimension $n$ as $\\mathbb{R}^n.$\n\nFor example, the following vectors are in $\\mathbb{R}, \\mathbb{R}^2,$ $\\mathbb{R}^3,$ and $\\mathbb{R}^5,$ respectively: \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{4} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nWe've already encountered several operations on vectors in $\\mathbb{R}^2$ or $\\mathbb{R}^3,$ namely, vector addition and subtraction, scalar multiplication, and the dot product. All of these operations work the same way in $\\mathbb{R}^n.$" 13194139534129 17592186045417 false] [17592186121033 1064 "In general, if we have two vectors $\\mathbf{u},\\mathbf{v}\\in\\mathbb{R}^n$ and a scalar $\\lambda\\in\\mathbb{R},$ then our vector operations are defined as follows:\n\n- The **sum/difference** of the two vectors is defined as\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1} \\\\ {u}_{2} \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ {u}_{n}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n- **Scalar multiplication** of a vector is defined as\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1} \\\\ {u}_{2} \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ {u}_{n}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n- The **dot product** of two vectors is defined as\n\n$$\n\\quad \\mathbf{u}\\cdot\\mathbf{v} = \\underbrace{u_1 \\cdot v_1 + u_2 \\cdot v_2 + \\cdots + u_n \\cdot v_n}_{\\Large{n} \\,\\textrm{-terms}} = \\sum_{i=1}^n u_i v_i.\n$$" 13194139534129 17592186045417 false] [17592186121033 1064 "In general, if we have two vectors $\\mathbf{u},\\mathbf{v}\\in\\mathbb{R}^n$ and a scalar $\\lambda\\in\\mathbb{R},$ then our vector operations are defined as follows:\n\n- The **sum/difference** of the two vectors is defined as\n![](images/fc/fc78b2f4e9262108a217e57fec67e096b8b8b18e1486bec8e51df60b8afdfdfa.svg)\n\n- **Scalar multiplication** of a vector is defined as\n![](images/e7/e78025f3481423365b2e924b6f8ba4e59f41e0c644ced7d2263238b219cba27a.svg)\n\n- The **dot product** of two vectors is defined as\n\n$$\n\\quad \\mathbf{u}\\cdot\\mathbf{v} = \\underbrace{u_1 \\cdot v_1 + u_2 \\cdot v_2 + \\cdots + u_n \\cdot v_n}_{\\Large{n} \\,\\textrm{-terms}} = \\sum_{i=1}^n u_i v_i.\n$$" 13194139534129 17592186045417 true] [17592186121062 1064 "To add two vectors in $\\mathbb{R}^n,$ we just need to add their corresponding components. To illustrate, consider the vectors $\\mathbf{a},\\mathbf{b} \\in \\mathbb{R}^4,$ given by\n\n![](images/1f/1f1b79f2e61fccbb27944284cbdd217a311a7f795100cfbb56340a457814b407.svg)\n\nTo compute $\\mathbf{a} + \\mathbf{b},$ all we have to do is to add each component of the vector $\\mathbf{a}$ with the corresponding component of $\\mathbf{b}.$ Doing this, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a+b & =[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}-5 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}5+(-5) \\\\ 1+3 \\\\ -3+1 \\\\ 6+0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can only compute the sum, difference, or dot product of two vectors if both vectors have the same number of components. For example, we can't find the following sum, so we say it's *undefined*:\n![](images/6c/6c377966aa9fa1d581c0f9e3ba16bceb4020471914d5c4fb61118ff7cd709466.svg)" 13194139534129 17592186045417 true] [17592186121062 1064 "To add two vectors in $\\mathbb{R}^n,$ we just need to add their corresponding components. To illustrate, consider the vectors $\\mathbf{a},\\mathbf{b} \\in \\mathbb{R}^4,$ given by\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nTo compute $\\mathbf{a} + \\mathbf{b},$ all we have to do is to add each component of the vector $\\mathbf{a}$ with the corresponding component of $\\mathbf{b}.$ Doing this, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a+b & =[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}-5 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}5+(-5) \\\\ 1+3 \\\\ -3+1 \\\\ 6+0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can only compute the sum, difference, or dot product of two vectors if both vectors have the same number of components. For example, we can't find the following sum, so we say it's *undefined*:\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]\n$$" 13194139534129 17592186045417 false] [17592186411199 1080 "23" 13194139534129 17592186045417 true] [17592186411199 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139534129 17592186045417 true] [17592186411199 1083 #uuid "088821a4-3897-5498-993d-4363e322c0fb" 13194139534129 17592186045417 true] [17592186411200 1080 "[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 11 \\\\ 12 \\\\ -10\\end{aligned}]" 13194139534129 17592186045417 true] [17592186411200 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139534129 17592186045417 true] [17592186411200 1083 #uuid "0eaa34a7-a085-545f-91fe-beae8d47c684" 13194139534129 17592186045417 true] [17592186411201 1080 "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{5} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{5} \\\\ \\frac{3}{5} \\\\ \\frac{2}{5} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]" 13194139534129 17592186045417 true] [17592186411201 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139534129 17592186045417 true] [17592186411201 1083 #uuid "119efa2d-2c30-5748-95ef-f1f